<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">15 December in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">5 events that happened on 15 December, from 37 to 2001.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">37</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The future emperor Nero is born at Antium</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus, later the emperor Nero, was born at Antium to Agrippina the Younger. Adopted by Claudius, he became emperor at 16 and was the last of the Julio-Claudian dynasty.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1791</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Bill of Rights is ratified</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Virginia's approval gave the first ten amendments to the US Constitution the required three-quarters of states. They guarantee freedoms of speech, religion, press and assembly, and rights to due process.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1890</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sitting Bull is killed at Standing Rock</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Lakota leader Sitting Bull, a leader at the victory over Custer at the Little Bighorn, was shot dead by Indian police trying to arrest him. Two weeks later came the massacre at Wounded Knee.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1939</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Gone with the Wind premieres in Atlanta</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The film of Margaret Mitchell's novel premiered at Loew's Grand Theatre in Atlanta. It won eight competitive Academy Awards, and allowing for inflation it remains the highest-grossing film ever.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">2001</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Leaning Tower of Pisa reopens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After eleven years of closure and stabilisation work, the Leaning Tower of Pisa reopened to visitors. Engineers had reduced its tilt by about 44 centimetres, securing it for centuries.</p></li></ol>
